<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Composting Is Easy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences. Reading: Identify themes in simple stories. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend short narratives about recycling by connecting them with personal experiences (e.g., "I recycle bottles like in the story."). Can identify themes in simple recycling-related stories and provide examples or connections from the story (e.g., "The story is about saving the Earth because the characters recycle bottles and plant trees."). Can segment and decode multisyllabic words into syllables (e.g., dividing "environment" into "en-vi-ron-ment").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Teacher shows flashcards of items: 'plastic bottle', 'food scraps', 'paper bag', 'recycling bin'. Students identify each item. Teacher asks: "What can we do with plastic bottles? (Recycle!) What can we do with food scraps? (Compost!)" This activates prior vocabulary from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher writes "recycle" and "environment" on the board. Teacher models the pronunciation of /r/ in "recycle" and /s/ in "sustainability" (e.g., by slowly saying 're-cy-cle' and 'sus-tain-a-bil-i-ty', exaggerating the sounds). Teacher then claps out the syllables for "en-vi-ron-ment" (en-vi-ron-ment) and "sus-tain-a-bil-i-ty" (sus-tain-a-bil-i-ty), pointing to the written word to connect sounds and graphemes. Students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'compost' trash for the landfill? (No, it's food for plants.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we throw plastic bottles in the regular trash? (No, we can recycle them.)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt titled "Leo's Green Garden" about a boy who starts composting food scraps and recycling plastic and paper with his family. The text will incorporate vocabulary: <i>compost, food scraps, plastic, bottles, paper, recycling bin, garden, environment, help, easy, clean, save</i>. It will use grammar: Present simple ("Leo composts."), Modal verbs ("We can help."), and There is/there are ("There is a compost bin.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences and identify themes in simple stories.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Teacher asks students to read the story silently and then asks: "What is the story mainly about? Is it about playing games or helping the Earth?" (Answer: Helping the Earth/Recycling/Composting).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Teacher provides 3-4 simple True/False statements about specific details from the text. Students read the statements and mark T or F.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo puts plastic bottles in the compost bin." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Composting helps the garden grow." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"There are many recycling centers in Leo's community." (True - based on implied context if not explicit).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying vocabulary (nouns: plastic, bottles, compost, recycling, trash, environment, food scraps, bin; verbs: recycle, compost, help, save; adjectives: easy, green, clean), grammar (Present simple, 'can', 'there is/are'), and phonemic awareness (segmenting multisyllabic words like "environment"). Pragmatic function: Asking questions about recycling ("What can we recycle?") and giving reasons ("We recycle to help the planet.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Reading Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from "Leo's Green Garden" that have missing words. They choose the correct word from a box (e.g., <i>compost, recycle, environment, food scraps</i>) to complete each sentence. This requires reading for context.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Leo puts his _______ in the green bin." (food scraps)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "We ______ plastic bottles to help the ______." (recycle, environment)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Reading - Identifying Themes & Word Parts:</b> Teacher writes two possible themes on the board: "A. How to play games" and "B. How to take care of our world." Students re-read the story and choose the main theme (B). Then, the teacher highlights words like "environment" and "sustainability" from the story or a related word list. Students practice segmenting these words into syllables by drawing lines between syllables in their notebooks (e.g., en | vi | ron | ment).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading & Checking:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of comprehension questions about "Leo's Green Garden" (e.g., "What does Leo do with plastic bottles?", "Is composting easy or difficult for Leo?"). One student reads a question, the other finds the answer in the text and reads the sentence containing the answer aloud. They take turns reading questions and finding answers, using present simple verbs and modal 'can' to describe actions in the story.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a different short, simple paragraph (2-3 sentences) related to composting or recycling (e.g., "Our school has a recycling program. We can put paper in the blue bin and plastic in the yellow bin. It is easy to help the environment.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Reading and Main Idea Identification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups read their paragraph together silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and writes down one sentence that identifies the main theme or idea of their paragraph (e.g., "This paragraph is about recycling paper and plastic at school." or "It explains how to compost food scraps at home.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They also identify one specific action mentioned in their text (e.g., "We can recycle paper.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then share their main idea sentence and action with the class, connecting it to the theme "Composting Is Easy" and the scenario "Recycling for Our World." Sociolinguistic competence: Discussing environmental actions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a short individual "Check for Understanding" quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short multiple-choice quiz based on "Leo's Green Garden" or one of the new paragraphs from Stage 4. Questions will assess:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Main idea comprehension (e.g., "What is the best title for this story?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Specific detail recall (e.g., "What does Leo put in the compost bin?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One question will ask students to underline the syllables in a multisyllabic word from the story (e.g., "environment").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying main ideas and specific details from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary when answering questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to segment multisyllabic words into syllables.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following sentences:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to read about _________."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading about recycling is (easy/a little difficult/difficult)."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the story was the most interesting to read?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I understand all the words in the story? Which words were new?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well today? (e.g., Students were engaged with the story, they could identify the main theme, segmentation practice was effective).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., provide more visual aids for specific vocabulary, spend more time on multi-syllabic word decoding, simplify question phrasing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How will today's reading practice connect to the next lesson? (Today, students practiced reading about composting and recycling actions. In Lesson 3, they will use this knowledge to speak and interact about these actions, using the vocabulary and structures learned).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>